# Dagster × OpenTelemetry — Integration Wiki

> Target: Dagster OSS on EKS. As of 2026-07 Dagster has **no first-party OTel integration**. Everything below is DIY, but all of it works.

---

## 0. TL;DR

| | Airflow | Dagster OSS |
|---|---|---|
| Official OTel **metrics** | ✅ built in (2.7+, `[metrics] otel_on = True`) | ❌ none |
| Official OTel **traces** | ✅ built in (2.10+, `[traces] otel_on`, experimental) | ❌ none |
| Official OTel **logs** | ⚠️ partial | ❌ none |
| Auto-instrumentation | works | works (it's just a Python process) |
| Upstream tracking | — | [#11191](https://github.com/dagster-io/dagster/issues/11191) (open since Dec 2022), [#12353](https://github.com/dagster-io/dagster/issues/12353) |

**Verdict: yes, you can — but there is no `otel_on = True` switch.** You build four layers:

| Layer | What | Code changes |
|---|---|---|
| **L0** Infrastructure | OTel Collector + k8s metadata + kube-state-metrics | none |
| **L1** Process instrumentation | `opentelemetry-instrument` on webserver / daemon / code servers | Dockerfile only |
| **L2** Business spans | tracer inside assets/ops, **context propagation via run tags** | pipeline code |
| **L3** Derived metrics | `run_status_sensor` → OTLP metrics | definitions code |

⚠️ **Naming trap:** Dagster's docs page at `docs.dagster.io/about/telemetry` describes **Dagster's own anonymous usage statistics** sent back to Dagster Labs. It has nothing to do with observability. `DAGSTER_DISABLE_TELEMETRY` is not the knob you're looking for.

---

## 1. Why Dagster is harder than Airflow

Airflow's scheduler is **one long-lived process** that every task lifecycle passes through. Instrument that one place and you cover everything. That's why Airflow could ship a config flag.

Dagster on Kubernetes is **aggressively distributed**:

```
dagster-webserver   (Starlette/uvicorn, GraphQL)  ──┐
dagster-daemon      (schedules, sensors,            │
                     run queue, backfills)       ───┼── Postgres (event log)
code location gRPC servers  (one per project)    ───┘   S3 (compute logs)
        │
        │  K8sRunLauncher creates a Kubernetes Job
        ▼
   Run Pod  ─ one per run, new process, new container
        │
        │  k8s_job_executor creates another Kubernetes Job per step
        ▼
   Step Pod ─ one per op/asset, lifetime can be < 5 seconds
```

Three consequences, all of which have solutions later in this doc:

1. **Trace context does not survive process boundaries.** The daemon triggers a run, the run pod executes it, step pods execute individual assets. That's three processes in three containers. This is the hard part and gets its own section (§5).
2. **Short-lived processes drop spans.** `BatchSpanProcessor` flushes every 5s by default. A step pod that runs for 3 seconds exits with its buffer full and unsent.
3. **Cardinality explodes if you're careless.** `run_id` is a UUID. Fine as a span attribute. Putting it on a metric will destroy your Prometheus/Mimir instance.

---

## 2. L0 — Infrastructure layer (do this first)

Highest value per unit of effort, and it's a prerequisite for everything else. Zero Dagster changes.

### 2.1 Collector topology

Two deployment options on EKS:

| Option | When |
|---|---|
| **OpenTelemetry Operator** | Recommended. CRD-managed Collectors, plus the `Instrumentation` CRD for zero-touch Python auto-injection (§3.2) |
| **ADOT (AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry)** | If your backend is CloudWatch / AMP / X-Ray — the EKS add-on path is smoother |

Use an **agent + gateway** split:

```
[Dagster pods] --OTLP/gRPC:4317--> [Collector DaemonSet: agent]
                                          │  k8sattributes, resourcedetection
                                          ▼
                                   [Collector Deployment: gateway]
                                          │  tail_sampling, batch
                                          ▼
                          Tempo / Jaeger / AMP / Datadog / SigNoz
```

The agent must be a **DaemonSet**, not a Deployment. The `k8sattributes` processor resolves pod IP → pod metadata, and step pods are so short-lived that you want the minimum number of network hops before the span is safely buffered off-pod.

### 2.2 Collector config skeleton

```yaml
receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc: { endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4317 }
      http: { endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318 }
  prometheus:
    config:
      scrape_configs:
        - job_name: kube-state-metrics
          static_configs:
            - targets: ['kube-state-metrics.kube-system:8080']

processors:
  k8sattributes:
    auth_type: serviceAccount
    passthrough: false
    extract:
      metadata:
        - k8s.namespace.name
        - k8s.pod.name
        - k8s.job.name          # Dagster run pods ARE Kubernetes Jobs — critical
        - k8s.node.name
      labels:
        - tag_name: dagster.run_id
          key: dagster/run-id
          from: pod
        - tag_name: dagster.code_location
          key: dagster/code-location
          from: pod
  resourcedetection:
    detectors: [env, eks, ec2]
  batch:
    timeout: 5s
    send_batch_size: 1024
  # Cardinality guard: strip UUID-ish attributes from METRICS only
  attributes/strip-cardinality:
    actions:
      - key: dagster.run_id
        action: delete
      - key: dagster.step_key
        action: delete

exporters:
  otlp/gateway:
    endpoint: otel-gateway.observability:4317
    tls: { insecure: true }

service:
  pipelines:
    traces:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [k8sattributes, resourcedetection, batch]
      exporters: [otlp/gateway]
    metrics:
      receivers: [otlp, prometheus]
      processors: [k8sattributes, resourcedetection, attributes/strip-cardinality, batch]
      exporters: [otlp/gateway]
    logs:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [k8sattributes, batch]
      exporters: [otlp/gateway]
```

### 2.3 What you get for free

Once the Collector is running, without touching Dagster at all:

| Signal | Source |
|---|---|
| Run pod CPU / memory / OOMKilled | kubelet + cAdvisor |
| Run failure | `kube_job_status_failed` (a failed run = a failed K8s Job) |
| Run queue latency | `kube_pod_status_phase` pending duration |
| Daemon liveness | pod readiness + daemon liveness probe |
| Event-log DB pressure | RDS Performance Insights / postgres receiver |

**This covers roughly 60% of real operational alerting.** Ship it before deciding whether the deeper layers are worth it.

